/** LIBREWOLF SETTINGS -- ANDROID
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * THIS IS A FRAGMENT, NOT A STANDALONE .cfg
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * It is never concatenated into librewolf.cfg. LW-M3-02's compiler
 * (scripts/cfg2prefs.py) reads common.cfg + android.cfg and emits the two
 * Android pref-delivery artifacts. Like desktop.cfg it has no sacrificial first
 * line, so it must never be handed to autoconfig on its own.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * DELIBERATELY EMPTY OF PREFS, and that is the honest result of LW-M3-01
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * The three-way split of librewolf.cfg produced 182 common prefs and 85 desktop
 * prefs and no Android-only pref. That is expected: librewolf.cfg is a
 * desktop-derived file, so the split can only ever *subtract* from it. Anything
 * Android-only has to be added by a task that has a reason to add it, and two
 * already exist:
 *
 *   LW-M3-06  maps settings/distribution/policies.json onto Android and appends
 *             the prefs that cover a policy here. It co-owns this file.
 *   LW-M5-01  locks fission.webContentIsolationStrategy. Whether that lands
 *             here or in the Kotlin layer is that task's call.
 *
 * Two things that look like they belong here and do not:
 *
 *   librewolf.webgl.prompt -- landmine L1. Already handled, and correctly, by
 *   patches/android/webgl-prompt-default.patch (assets/patches/android.txt),
 *   which flips the compiled-in default. Do not restate it as a pref here; two
 *   sources for one value is how they drift.
 *
 *   The ~50-pref must-lock list -- that is LW-M3-04, generated mechanically
 *   from GeckoRuntimeSettings.java and ContentBlocking.java. common.cfg carries
 *   `[ANDROID: LOCK]` markers on the 22 prefs we ship that those two files
 *   declare today; they are a cross-reference for LW-M3-04, not the list.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * TWO DELIVERY FACTS LW-M3-02 AND LW-M3-03 MUST NOT ASSUME AWAY
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * 1. MOZ_DEFAULT_PREFS cannot lock. GeckoLoader.java:83-103 builds a string of
 *    `pref("name",value);` lines; Preferences.cpp:3963 hands it to
 *    parsePrefData(..., PrefValueKind::Default); and the grammar that parser
 *    accepts is `pref` / `user_pref` / `sticky_pref` only
 *    (modules/libpref/parser/src/lib.rs:14, 297-321). There is no `locked_pref`
 *    token. Every one of the 38 lockPref calls in common.cfg needs a real
 *    Preferences::Lock on Android, wired by LW-M3-03.
 *
 * 2. MOZ_DEFAULT_PREFS writes the DEFAULT branch, but autoconfig's bare
 *    `pref()` writes the USER branch (prefcalls.js:12-34, getPrefBranch() ==
 *    Services.prefs.getBranch(null)) and does so at every startup. The 24 bare
 *    pref() calls in common.cfg are exactly the ones whose comments say the
 *    value "won't stick" as a default. Compiling them into MOZ_DEFAULT_PREFS is
 *    a silent downgrade: a stale user value in the profile then wins.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * GAP REGISTER
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * Prefs that went to desktop.cfg because the *implementation* is desktop-only,
 * while the *behaviour* is still something LibreWolf for Android should have.
 * Each needs a non-pref answer. These feed the M5 parity matrix; do not treat
 * an entry here as closed just because the pref moved.
 *
 *   G1  Sanitize on shutdown
 *       desktop.cfg: privacy.sanitize.* + privacy.clearOnShutdown_v2.*
 *       Sanitizer.sys.mjs is browser/modules/. Fenix has "Delete browsing data
 *       on quit" in Kotlin, keyed off its own SharedPreferences. Owner: M4/M5.
 *
 *   G2  Account and sync
 *       desktop.cfg: identity.fxaccounts.enabled
 *       services/fxaccounts is not built on Android and MOZ_SERVICES_SYNC is
 *       browser-only. Fenix's stack is Kotlin over application-services. Owner: M4.
 *
 *   G3  First-run and messaging surfaces
 *       desktop.cfg: startup.homepage_welcome_url*, browser.uitour.*,
 *       startup.homepage_override_nimbus_disable_wnp, the four
 *       activity-stream.asrouter.providers.* locks
 *       Fenix onboarding + Nimbus Messaging are Kotlin. Owner: M4.
 *
 *   G4  Home-screen telemetry and sponsored content
 *       desktop.cfg: browser.newtabpage.activity-stream.*, browser.topsites.*
 *       Fenix home screen, Contile sponsored tiles, Pocket -- all Kotlin. Owner: M4.
 *
 *   G5  Web-compat reporter
 *       desktop.cfg: extensions.webcompat-reporter.*
 *       Android ships android-components feature/webcompat-reporter, a
 *       different extension installed from Kotlin. Owner: M4.
 *
 *   G6  Search-suggestion and urlbar surfaces
 *       desktop.cfg: browser.urlbar.*
 *       browser.search.suggest.enabled (common.cfg) covers the network side.
 *       The Fenix awesomebar's own suggestion providers, and its speculative
 *       connections (Engine.speculativeConnect, which no pref gates), do not.
 *       Owner: M4.
 *
 * NOT A GAP, and it must stay off this list:
 *
 *   privacy.resistFingerprinting.letterboxing. Desktop LibreWolf ships it
 *   FALSE (see desktop.cfg and docs/android/ROADMAP.md). Android not having
 *   letterboxing costs our users nothing. Earlier drafts of the plan listed it
 *   as a parity item; that was wrong.
 */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * ANDROID-ONLY PREFS
 * LW-M3-06 appends below this line. Keep the file parseable when it is empty.
 * --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * FROM settings/distribution/policies.json  (LW-M3-06)
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * The enterprise policy engine does not exist on Android, and it is gated
 * twice: toolkit/components/enterprisepolicies/moz.build:16 puts both the
 * EXTRA_JS_MODULES and XPCOM_MANIFESTS += ["components.conf"] inside
 * `if CONFIG["MOZ_WIDGET_TOOLKIT"] != "android"`, and every policy body lives
 * in browser/components/enterprisepolicies/Policies.sys.mjs, which Android
 * never builds. components.conf is what declares js_name 'policies', so
 * `Services.policies` is UNDEFINED on Android -- not merely inactive. Toolkit
 * code that runs on both already guards for exactly that (ResetProfile.sys.mjs:56,
 * UpdateUtils.sys.mjs:498, ServiceRequest.sys.mjs:88).
 *
 * All 22 policy keys are mapped in docs/android/POLICIES.md. Only TWO of them
 * reduce to a pref that is (a) absent from common.cfg and desktop.cfg, (b) read
 * by code that ships on Android, and (c) not already at the policy's value by
 * default. Those two are below.
 *
 * The other 20 are deliberately NOT here. Twelve need Kotlin-layer work and are
 * registered as gaps P1..P7 in POLICIES.md; the remaining eight are moot on
 * Android (no updater, no default-browser agent, no browser/ chrome, or the
 * Android default already equals the policy value). Restating a
 * desktop-only pref here would parse fine, change nothing, and show up green in
 * scripts/android-pref-audit.sh -- which is exactly the hole this task exists
 * to avoid.
 *
 * BRANCH AND LOCK. Both prefs below are `defaultPref`, i.e. DEFAULT branch,
 * UNLOCKED. That is not a compromise forced by the delivery channel -- it is
 * what the desktop policy does. PoliciesUtils.setDefaultPref
 * (Policies.sys.mjs:3449-3495) writes Services.prefs.getDefaultBranch("") and
 * locks only when its third argument is truthy; both of these policies pass
 * `param.Locked`, and neither policies.json entry has a Locked key. A lockPref
 * here would be STRICTER than desktop, not equal to it.
 *
 * Consequently neither pref depends on LW-M3-08. MOZ_DEFAULT_PREFS writes the
 * default branch, so it carries both correctly today; native autoconfig would
 * carry them identically. Neither appears in GeckoRuntimeSettings.java,
 * ContentBlocking.java, or the Nimbus gecko-pref map in
 * mobile/android/fenix/app/nimbus.fml.yaml, so no runtime writer overwrites
 * them and no lock is needed to make them stick.
 *
 * Note for LW-M3-04: that Nimbus map is a THIRD pref writer, beyond the two in
 * landmine L2 -- NimbusGeckoPrefHandler.kt:54,283 applies remotely-fetched
 * values via setGeckoPrefsState(). It is registered as P8 in POLICIES.md.
 */

/** EncryptedMediaExtensions: {"Enabled": false}
 *
 * The only policy row where Android's own default actively disagrees with
 * LibreWolf. StaticPrefList.yaml:12163-12176 guards the value with
 * `#if defined(XP_LINUX) && !defined(MOZ_WIDGET_ANDROID)` -- Android takes the
 * #else and gets TRUE. Desktop LibreWolf ships EME off via this policy alone;
 * media.eme.enabled is in neither common.cfg nor desktop.cfg. Without the line
 * below, LibreWolf for Android would ship DRM enabled while the desktop build
 * ships it disabled.
 *
 * Android's media.eme.require-app-approval (geckoview-prefs.js:287, honoured at
 * MediaKeySystemAccessManager.cpp:259) adds a per-site embedder prompt on top,
 * but a prompt is not the blanket disable the policy expresses.
 */
defaultPref("media.eme.enabled", false);

/** LocalNetworkAccess: {"Enabled": true, "BlockTrackers": true, "EnablePrompting": true}
 *
 * Two of the three sub-keys already match the Android default:
 *   network.lna.enabled   policy true  == StaticPrefList.yaml:15373-15376 true
 *   network.lna.blocking  policy true  == StaticPrefList.yaml:15379-15382 true  (EnablePrompting)
 *   network.lna.block_trackers  policy true  != StaticPrefList.yaml:15386-15389 FALSE
 *
 * so only block_trackers needs stating. common.cfg:280-282 already ships three
 * neighbouring network.lna.* prefs but not this one -- desktop gets it from the
 * policy.
 *
 * The prompting half is functional on Android: GeckoSession.java:7213 defines
 * PERMISSION_LOCAL_NETWORK_ACCESS ("local-network" at :7365,:7394) and
 * android-components persists it (SitePermissions.kt:31,97).
 *
 * [ANDROID: LOCK] GeckoRuntimeSettings.java:789
 * CORRECTION (skeptical verification of LW-M3-06): an earlier version of this
 * comment and of docs/android/POLICIES.md asserted this pref is "not present in
 * GeckoRuntimeSettings.java ... so no writer overwrites it and no lock is
 * needed". That is FALSE. It is declared at GeckoRuntimeSettings.java:789 as
 * `new PrefWithoutDefault<>("network.lna.block_trackers")`, which makes it
 * exactly the landmine-L2 case: GeckoView owns it at runtime, so an unlocked
 * defaultPref here is transient. It needs a real lock, and per LW-M3-01 the
 * MOZ_DEFAULT_PREFS channel cannot express one -- so the value below is
 * ADVISORY until LW-M3-08 settles whether autoconfig (and therefore lockPref)
 * works on Android. LW-M3-04 must pick this pref up from the generated list.
 */
defaultPref("network.lna.block_trackers", true);

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * FROM settings/common.cfg's BARE pref() CALLS  (LW-M3-09, landmine L2b)
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * A bare `pref()` in a .cfg writes the USER branch
 * (extensions/pref/autoconfig/src/prefcalls.js:12-18 routes it through
 * Services.prefs.getBranch(null)), and on Android
 * `GeckoView:ResetUserPrefs` runs AFTER autoconfig and clears the user branch:
 * GeckoViewStartup.sys.mjs:394-398 loops `Services.prefs.clearUserPref(name)`
 * over a name list built by RuntimeSettings.java:353-367 from `forAllPrefs`.
 *
 * So the question for each of common.cfg's 24 bare pref() calls is exactly one
 * question: IS THE NAME IN THAT LIST? The list is the prefs declared by the
 * three RuntimeSettings subclasses -- GeckoRuntimeSettings (GeckoRuntimeSettings.java:34),
 * ContentBlocking.Settings (ContentBlocking.java:104) and SafeBrowsingProvider
 * (ContentBlocking.java:1571) -- minus those that neither hasDefault() nor
 * isSet(). 101 names are declared with literal strings; SafeBrowsingProvider
 * builds 12 more per provider from `ROOT + mName + "..."` (:1815-1826) for
 * google, google4 and google5, which is 36 names no literal-string grep sees.
 *
 * FOUR of the 24 are in that list. The other TWENTY are not, so their user
 * value survives on Android exactly as it does on desktop and they are
 * deliberately NOT restated here -- promoting them would be a REGRESSION, not a
 * fix: a bare pref() re-asserts the value at every startup and overrides a
 * stale profile value, and a defaultPref would let that stale value win. The
 * per-pref table and the measurements are in docs/android/parity/bare-prefs.md.
 *
 * common.cfg is UNCHANGED by this task, byte for byte. Desktop keeps the bare
 * pref() semantics it needs -- for the category pref, common.cfg:113 says so in
 * as many words ("the desired category must be set with pref() otherwise it
 * won't stick") -- and settings/librewolf.cfg therefore does not move either.
 *
 * Measured on the built APK, not inferred: 21 of the 24 already read
 * LibreWolf's value; 3 did not. See bare-prefs.md for the run.
 *
 * WHY defaultPref FOR SOME AND lockPref FOR OTHERS. ResetUserPrefs only clears
 * the user branch, so defaultPref is enough to beat it. lockPref is needed only
 * where a SECOND writer exists: `Pref.commit()` (RuntimeSettings.java:113-122)
 * dispatches GeckoView:SetDefaultPrefs at RUNTIME, which rewrites the default
 * branch and beats an unlocked defaultPref (landmine L2). A lock is not free --
 * it makes the Fenix control that writes the pref inert -- so it is used only
 * where a runtime writer was actually found, and each one is named below for
 * LW-M3-04's must-not-lock review.
 */

/** browser.contentblocking.category -- common.cfg:117, bare pref("strict")
 *
 * THE MEASURED CASUALTY. about:config on Android read "standard": GeckoView
 * commits its own Java default (ContentBlocking.java:579, "standard") to the
 * DEFAULT branch, our "strict" went to the user branch, and ResetUserPrefs
 * removed it. Android was shipping Fenix's tracking-protection category while
 * the config said strict, and no build error showed it.
 *
 * This is NOT a cosmetic desktop-UI aggregate on Android. It is read by code
 * that ships here: netwerk/url-classifier/UrlClassifierExceptionListEntry.cpp:99-112
 * skips a Remote Settings tracking-protection EXCEPTION entry unless its
 * `filterContentBlockingCategories` list contains this string -- so "standard"
 * activates the standard-category exceptions, i.e. allow-lists trackers that
 * "strict" would keep blocked. Also read at
 * toolkit/components/url-classifier/RealTimeRequestSimulator.cpp:26.
 *
 * lockPref, not defaultPref: there is a runtime writer.
 * ContentBlocking.java:1062-1073 setEnhancedTrackingProtectionCategory calls
 * mEtpCategory.commit(...), reached from GeckoEngine.kt:1511 in the
 * trackingProtectionPolicy setter, which Fenix drives from
 * TrackingProtectionFragment.kt. commit() early-returns only while the Java
 * value is unchanged, so an unlocked defaultPref survives exactly until the
 * user touches the ETP screen.
 *
 * COST, and it is a real one: Fenix's Settings > Enhanced Tracking Protection
 * category selector becomes inert -- it will still move, and the behaviour will
 * not. That is what desktop does too (LibreWolf hides the equivalent UI and
 * rewrites the pref at every startup), so this is parity, not a new decision --
 * but it belongs in LW-M3-04's "locked, known to leave a visible control dead"
 * list, not in the must-not-lock allowlist.
 */
lockPref("browser.contentblocking.category", "strict");

/** devtools.debugger.remote-enabled -- common.cfg:561, bare pref(false)
 *
 * Reads false on Android today, and only by coincidence: GeckoView's own Java
 * default is false too (GeckoRuntimeSettings.java:748). Our user-branch value
 * is wiped just the same, so the correct value is being supplied by Fenix, not
 * by us -- it would flip silently if that default ever changed.
 *
 * lockPref, not defaultPref: SettingsFragment.kt:603 assigns
 * engine.settings.remoteDebuggingEnabled at RUNTIME from the "Remote debugging
 * via USB" switch, which is mRemoteDebugging.commit(...). LW-M3-08 measured
 * this exact pair on a running build: lockPref'd, it stayed false after the
 * switch was turned ON, while an unlocked control pref flipped in the same
 * session and process. That is the observation landmine L2 is built on.
 *
 * COST: the "Remote debugging via USB" switch becomes inert. Desktop LibreWolf
 * ships remote debugging off, so this is parity. For LW-M3-04's dead-control
 * list.
 */
lockPref("devtools.debugger.remote-enabled", false);

/** devtools.console.stdout.chrome -- common.cfg:166, bare pref(false)
 *
 * Reads TRUE on Android. GeckoRuntimeSettings.java:767 declares it with a Java
 * default of true, GeckoProvider.kt:121 feeds it from Fenix's hidden
 * enableGeckoLogs setting, and ResetUserPrefs removed our false. So chrome
 * console output goes to logcat on Android while LibreWolf disables it
 * everywhere else -- see the common.cfg:161-164 rationale (these are off in
 * official Mozilla builds).
 *
 * defaultPref is enough, and lockPref would be over-locking. There is NO
 * runtime writer: mConsoleOutput is only reached through the Builder
 * (GeckoRuntimeSettings.java:224-225, `set()` not `commit()`), so its value
 * arrives before the profile via getPrefsMap/MOZ_DEFAULT_PREFS and autoconfig
 * runs after it. setConsoleOutputEnabled (:1588) has no caller in Fenix, and
 * flipping the secret-menu toggle (SettingsFragment.kt:726-737) only writes a
 * SharedPreference and calls exitProcess(0), so the value is re-derived through
 * the Builder on the next launch. Leaving it unlocked also keeps the rest of
 * that debug toggle (geckoview.logging) working.
 * [ANDROID: LOCK] GeckoRuntimeSettings.java:767
 */
defaultPref("devtools.console.stdout.chrome", false);

/** browser.safebrowsing.provider.google4.dataSharingURL -- common.cfg:384, bare pref("")
 *
 * Reads
 * https://safebrowsing.googleapis.com/v4/threatHits?$ct=application/x-protobuf&key=%GOOGLE_SAFEBROWSING_API_KEY%&$httpMethod=POST
 * on Android. `browser.safebrowsing.provider.google4.dataSharing.enabled` is
 * false so nothing is sent today, but blanking the URL is the defence in depth
 * the common.cfg comment asks for, and it is not in place.
 *
 * This name is NOT one of the 101 literal declarations. SafeBrowsingProvider
 * builds it as ROOT + mName + ".dataSharingURL" (ContentBlocking.java:1572,
 * :1824) and GOOGLE_SAFE_BROWSING_PROVIDER supplies a value (:70-72), which
 * makes isSet() true and puts it in the reset list. Every name-literal grep --
 * including board.py's _gv_declared_prefs, and therefore its
 * BARE_PREF_BACKLOG ratchet -- misses all 36 of these.
 *
 * defaultPref is enough, and this is measured rather than argued: the four
 * sibling provider URLs common.cfg already ships as defaultPref
 * (google/google4 gethashURL + updateURL, common.cfg:371-374) are declared by
 * the same mechanism, are equally isSet by the default providers, and all four
 * read "" on the same build. Fenix never rebuilds the provider configuration at
 * runtime, so no commit() reaches these.
 * [ANDROID: LOCK] ContentBlocking.java:1824 (dynamic name)
 */
defaultPref("browser.safebrowsing.provider.google4.dataSharingURL", "");
